      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783168016349,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Core Entities\n- Item\n- Inventory\n- Distribution Center\n- Order\n- Order Item",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Core Entities\n- Item\n- Inventory\n- Distribution Center\n- Order\n- Order Item",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "sl44vsB0Begy53fRFL3KG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -11838.555696794478,
      "y": 28709.797970749387,
      "width": 807.2794189453125,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 807420307,
      "version": 344,
      "versionNonce": 1644232509,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783168568415,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "APIs\n\n// availability of items\nGET /v1/availability?lat=LAT&long=LONG&keyword={}&page_size={}&page_num={}\nReturns -> \n{\n    items: {\n        name: NAME,\n        quantity: QTY\n    }[]\n}\n\n// place an order\nPOST /v1/order\n{\n    lat: LAT,\n    long: LONG,\n    items: ITEM1, ITEM2, ITEM3...\n    ....\n  } -> Order | Failure",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "APIs\n\n// availability of items\nGET /v1/availability?lat=LAT&long=LONG&keyword={}&page_size={}&page_num={}\nReturns -> \n{\n    items: {\n        name: NAME,\n        quantity: QTY\n    }[]\n}\n\n// place an order\nPOST /v1/order\n{\n    lat: LAT,\n    long: LONG,\n    items: ITEM1, ITEM2, ITEM3...\n    ....\n  } -> Order | Failure",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Ffr5XRm0-6nRYzwYukS9B",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -12297.042123437916,
      "y": 29302.703920884396,
      "width": 1272.479248046875,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 606795347,
      "version": 869,
      "versionNonce": 1456812061,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783169779300,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Notes\n\n1. Query Availability of Items by location\n    - find all the DCs that 1H away\n    - Query on the resulting DC list for inventory and union all of them and return to user\n    - Build a simple internal API that takes LAT & LONG and return list of DC 1H away\n    - Have a DC table to store all DCs with their lat and long.\n    - Once DC list available query Inventory and Items table by joining them as we're using Postgres DB\n    - Note : Many e-commerce providers store catalog separately from the inventory because of different consumers and workloads\n      We're using same DB for now, if prompted we'll add a search index(like elasticsearch) to allow searching the catalog etc. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Notes\n\n1. Query Availability of Items by location\n    - find all the DCs that 1H away\n    - Query on the resulting DC list for inventory and union all of them and return to user\n    - Build a simple internal API that takes LAT & LONG and return list of DC 1H away\n    - Have a DC table to store all DCs with their lat and long.\n    - Once DC list available query Inventory and Items table by joining them as we're using Postgres DB\n    - Note : Many e-commerce providers store catalog separately from the inventory because of different consumers and workloads\n      We're using same DB for now, if prompted we'll add a search index(like elasticsearch) to allow searching the catalog etc. ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "XAWfXJ7Of0j2w48LH7VdJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -12266.571582103354,
      "y": 29554.085886894474,
      "width": 1046.779296875,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1137972499,
      "version": 41,
      "versionNonce": 299065789,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783169990282,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\nTo sum up:\n   1. We make a request to the Availability Service with the user's location X and Y and any relevant filters.\n   2. The availability service fires a request to the Nearby Service with the user's location X and Y.\n   3. The nearby service returns us a list of DCs that can deliver to our location.\n   4. With the DCs available, the availability service query our database with those DC IDs.\n   5. We sum up the results and return them to our client.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\nTo sum up:\n   1. We make a request to the Availability Service with the user's location X and Y and any relevant filters.\n   2. The availability service fires a request to the Nearby Service with the user's location X and Y.\n   3. The nearby service returns us a list of DCs that can deliver to our location.\n   4. With the DCs available, the availability service query our database with those DC IDs.\n   5. We sum up the results and return them to our client.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "exN2ZhWf68HAwg-xJblDV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -12286.543077864135,
      "y": 29785.05993011948,
      "width": 2009.618896484375,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 279292275,
      "version": 1370,
      "versionNonce": 253661235,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783170905337,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. Customers should be able to order items\n- Strong Consistency - don't want 2 users ordering the same item, latency is tolerable at this stage than at searching\n- Good Solution : Two data stores with a distributed lock\n    - Separate databases for orders and inventory\n    - when placing an order lock the relevant inventory record\n    - create the order record\n    - decrement the inventory\n    - release the lock\n    - Separating DB allows us using key-value for inventory and relational for orders\n    \n    - Problems : what if service crashes after we created order but failed to decrement the inventory\n        - What if two orders have overlapping inventory requirements? e.g. u1 and u2 both trying to buy A & B, u1 has lock on A and u2 has lock on B -> Deadlock\n \n - Great Solution : Singular Postgres txn\n    - putting both orders and inventory to same DB and taking adv of ACID\n    - A singular txn with isolation level SERIALIZABLE ensuring entire txn is atomic\n    - Meaning 2 users trying to order same item at same time, one will be rejected\n    - Problem : Partly coupling the scaling of inventory and orders\n\n    To sum up:\n        1. The user makes a request to the Orders Service to place an order for items A, B, and C.\n        2. The Orders Service makes creates a singular transaction which we submit to our Postgres leader. This transaction: a. Checks the inventory for items A, B, and C > 0.\n            b. If any of the items are out of stock, the transaction fails. c. If all items are in stock, the transaction records the order and updates the status for inventory items A, B, and C to \"ordered\". \n            d. A new row is created in the Orders table (and OrderItems table) recording the order for A, B, and C. e. The transaction is committed.\n        3. If the transaction succeeds, we return the order to the user.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. Customers should be able to order items\n- Strong Consistency - don't want 2 users ordering the same item, latency is tolerable at this stage than at searching\n- Good Solution : Two data stores with a distributed lock\n    - Separate databases for orders and inventory\n    - when placing an order lock the relevant inventory record\n    - create the order record\n    - decrement the inventory\n    - release the lock\n    - Separating DB allows us using key-value for inventory and relational for orders\n    \n    - Problems : what if service crashes after we created order but failed to decrement the inventory\n        - What if two orders have overlapping inventory requirements? e.g. u1 and u2 both trying to buy A & B, u1 has lock on A and u2 has lock on B -> Deadlock\n \n - Great Solution : Singular Postgres txn\n    - putting both orders and inventory to same DB and taking adv of ACID\n    - A singular txn with isolation level SERIALIZABLE ensuring entire txn is atomic\n    - Meaning 2 users trying to order same item at same time, one will be rejected\n    - Problem : Partly coupling the scaling of inventory and orders\n\n    To sum up:\n        1. The user makes a request to the Orders Service to place an order for items A, B, and C.\n        2. The Orders Service makes creates a singular transaction which we submit to our Postgres leader. This transaction: a. Checks the inventory for items A, B, and C > 0.\n            b. If any of the items are out of stock, the transaction fails. c. If all items are in stock, the transaction records the order and updates the status for inventory items A, B, and C to \"ordered\". \n            d. A new row is created in the Orders table (and OrderItems table) recording the order for A, B, and C. e. The transaction is committed.\n        3. If the transaction succeeds, we return the order to the user.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
