      "index": "ap",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1524488691,
      "version": 162,
      "versionNonce": 391243667,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783172989369,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Postgres DB partioned by region\n- Availability service reads via read replicas\n- Orders Service writes to leader using atomic txns",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Postgres DB partioned by region\n- Availability service reads via read replicas\n- Orders Service writes to leader using atomic txns",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "9fyjJtRmdc80OdH4JXMnb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -10280.381052332936,
      "y": 28922.89626528267,
      "width": 160.31587380800335,
      "height": 36.97324623341048,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aq",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1846021235,
      "version": 121,
      "versionNonce": 404345917,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783175244880,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Deep Dives",
      "fontSize": 29.57859698672838,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Deep Dives",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "3vW55ks7JcmB1w0miDmP3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -10274.564826458642,
      "y": 29006.66354965191,
      "width": 1283.359130859375,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ar",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1544793533,
      "version": 1238,
      "versionNonce": 900613277,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783175244880,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Make availability looks incorporate traffic and drive time\n   - Currently we opted for simple euclidean distance but that is incorrect as location could be unreachable by road, drive time more\n      and we want 1H drive time reachable\n   1. Bad Solution : the one we used!\n   2. Bad Solution : Use Travel Time Estimation Service against all DCs\n        - DCs are fixed, sync periodically(5 mins) from a DC table to the memory of our service\n        - i.e. always store pre-calculated time to all DCs from user\n        - Using a separate travel time estimation service for this.\n        - Problem : far too many unnecessary queries, most of the DCs we're querying aren't even close to us\n   3. Great Solution : Use Travel Time Estimation Service against nearby DCs\n        - same as above but instead of calculating our distance from all DCs we take a fixed radius say 60miles (1H drivable)\n        - send these candidates to the Travel Time Estimation service and select the best one.\n\n ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Make availability looks incorporate traffic and drive time\n   - Currently we opted for simple euclidean distance but that is incorrect as location could be unreachable by road, drive time more\n      and we want 1H drive time reachable\n   1. Bad Solution : the one we used!\n   2. Bad Solution : Use Travel Time Estimation Service against all DCs\n        - DCs are fixed, sync periodically(5 mins) from a DC table to the memory of our service\n        - i.e. always store pre-calculated time to all DCs from user\n        - Using a separate travel time estimation service for this.\n        - Problem : far too many unnecessary queries, most of the DCs we're querying aren't even close to us\n   3. Great Solution : Use Travel Time Estimation Service against nearby DCs\n        - same as above but instead of calculating our distance from all DCs we take a fixed radius say 60miles (1H drivable)\n        - send these candidates to the Travel Time Estimation service and select the best one.\n\n ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "iO5QN5OzDPb3NYge4_ZZ0",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -10233.536849531843,
      "y": 29375.121458788515,
      "width": 1651.518798828125,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "as",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1347457373,
      "version": 1726,
      "versionNonce": 471181565,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783175244880,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Make availability lookups fast and scalable\n    - Try to introduce estimations when you identify a bottleneck in your systems\n    \n        10Mil orders/day / (100k seconds/day) * 10(pages scrolled/searched by 1 user)/ 0.05(Only 5% end up odering rest just scroll) = 20k queries/sec\n    \n    Definitely needs scaling, requires aggressive caching with short TTLs\n    \n    1. Great Solution : Query Inventory through Cache\n        - Add Redis to our design\n        - Availability service queries cache if hit return\n        - if miss, lookup DB and write data back to cache and return to user\n        - Set a low TTL(1 min) ensures the results are fresh.\n        - Problems : Order service needs to expire affected cache when it writes to inventory table\n    \n    2. Great Solution : Postgres Read Replicas and Partitioning\n        - We read from nearby DCs, we group them together with region ID using first 3 digits of zipcode\n        - Partition the inventory based on this regionID, this means all our queries will go to mostly 1 or 2 partitions rather than entire inventory dataset\n        - Add read replicas for availability as we can tolerate small read inconsistency but our writes need to go to Postgres leader and availability query goes to replicas\n        - Problems : Manage partitioning or our data to avoid hot partitioning, manage sizing replicas to balance with traffic",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Make availability lookups fast and scalable\n    - Try to introduce estimations when you identify a bottleneck in your systems\n    \n        10Mil orders/day / (100k seconds/day) * 10(pages scrolled/searched by 1 user)/ 0.05(Only 5% end up odering rest just scroll) = 20k queries/sec\n    \n    Definitely needs scaling, requires aggressive caching with short TTLs\n    \n    1. Great Solution : Query Inventory through Cache\n        - Add Redis to our design\n        - Availability service queries cache if hit return\n        - if miss, lookup DB and write data back to cache and return to user\n        - Set a low TTL(1 min) ensures the results are fresh.\n        - Problems : Order service needs to expire affected cache when it writes to inventory table\n    \n    2. Great Solution : Postgres Read Replicas and Partitioning\n        - We read from nearby DCs, we group them together with region ID using first 3 digits of zipcode\n        - Partition the inventory based on this regionID, this means all our queries will go to mostly 1 or 2 partitions rather than entire inventory dataset\n        - Add read replicas for availability as we can tolerate small read inconsistency but our writes need to go to Postgres leader and availability query goes to replicas\n        - Problems : Manage partitioning or our data to avoid hot partitioning, manage sizing replicas to balance with traffic",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
